-- title:
--   Perfectness of the Cartier pairing on Tate modules
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, $p$ a prime and $h$ a natural number, and let $G$, $G'$ be $p$-divisible groups of height $h$ over $R$ in the Hopf-algebraic sense used here: each is given by commutative cocommutative Hopf $R$-algebras $G.\mathrm{level}\,v$ that are finite free of rank $p^{vh}$ over $R$, together with surjective coalgebra-algebra transition maps $G.\mathrm{level}(v+1)\to G.\mathrm{level}\,v$ whose kernel is the ideal generated by the image of the augmentation ideal under multiplication by $p^{v}$. Let $D$ be a Cartier duality datum between $G$ and $G'$: for each $v$ an isomorphism $G'.\mathrm{level}\,v\cong \mathrm{Hom}_R(G.\mathrm{level}\,v,R)$ of bialgebras, compatible with the transitions in the sense that pairing a transition of $G'$ against a transition of $G$ agrees with pairing at level $v+1$ after applying multiplication by $p$ on $G.\mathrm{level}(v+1)$. Let $L$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero that is an $R$-algebra. For an abelian group $M$, $\mathrm{TateModule}\;p\;M$ is the group of sequences $(x_n)_{n\in\mathbb N}$ in $M$ with $p^{n}x_n=0$ and $p\,x_{n+1}=x_n$. Let $B$ be a $\mathbb Z_p$-bilinear map from $\mathrm{TateModule}\,p\,(G.\mathrm{Points}\,L)$ and $\mathrm{TateModule}\,p\,(G'.\mathrm{Points}\,L)$ to $\mathrm{TateModule}\,p\,(\mathrm{Additive}\,L^{\times})$, where $G.\mathrm{Points}\,L$ is the direct limit over $v$ of the groups of $R$-algebra maps $G.\mathrm{level}\,v\to L$ under convolution. Assume that for all $x$, $y$, all $v$, and all points $f$ of $G$ and $\psi$ of $G'$ at level $v$ whose images in the direct limit are the $v$-th components of $x$ and of $y$ respectively, the $v$-th component of $B\,x\,y$, viewed in $L$, equals $D.\mathrm{pair}\,L\,v\,f\,\psi=\sum_i f(b_i)\,\psi\bigl(\theta_v^{-1}(b.\mathrm{coord}\,i)\bigr)$, for the chosen $R$-basis $b$ of $G.\mathrm{level}\,v$ and the duality isomorphism $\theta_v$ attached to $D$. Then both $B$ and its flip are bijective, that is, each of $x\mapsto B(x,\cdot)$ and $y\mapsto B(\cdot,y)$ is a bijection onto the full $\mathbb Z_p$-module of $\mathbb Z_p$-linear maps into $\mathrm{TateModule}\,p\,(\mathrm{Additive}\,L^{\times})$.
--
--   This is the perfectness of the limit Cartier pairing, i.e. Tate's identification $T(G')\cong\mathrm{Hom}_{\mathbb Z_p}(T(G),\mathbb Z_p(1))$ for a $p$-divisible group and its dual over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero. It is used to transfer statements about submodules, endomorphisms and Galois action from $T(G)$ to $T(G')$, and feeds into the analysis of the Tate module of the Jacobian of a modular curve at an ordinary prime.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_PDivisibleGroup_CartierDuality_bijective_tateModule_pairing_of_isAlgClosed.lean

